# rules: java-weak-crypto
# fixture: java/weak-crypto.java
# from: java/lang/security/audit/crypto/use-of-md5.yaml (use-of-md5), use-of-sha1.yaml (use-of-sha1), use-of-aes-ecb.yaml (use-of-aes-ecb)
#
# CWE-327 and CWE-328. Three upstream rules with one shape between them: the
# algorithm is a string handed to getInstance, so the pattern finds the call
# and a regex decides whether the string names something broken.
#
# That is `metavariable-regex`, and it is the operator the port leans on
# hardest, because Java says what it wants in a string where Go and Python say
# it in the name of the function. It is also the operator that needs a hole to
# have caught the right thing: with $ALGO bound to the argument list rather
# than the argument, the regex would be testing "(\"MD5\")" and an anchor would
# quietly stop matching.
#
# ECB mode is in the same rule rather than its own because it is the same
# sentence - a cipher named by a string that says how it will fail.

["$CLASS.getInstance($ALGO)"] as $chosen
| scan_ast("*.java"; $chosen)
| where_capture("ALGO"; "MD5|SHA-?1[^0-9]|/ECB/")
| finding("java-weak-crypto";
    "$ALGO names an algorithm with known breaks; SHA-256 for a digest, "
    + "AES/GCM/NoPadding for a cipher")
| report
